<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 921–922</span> · Also known as Ibn Fadlan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad ibn Fadlan was an Arab member of the embassy sent by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Muqtadir to the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> in 921 to 922. His account describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, the Bulgars' conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and a Rus' ship burial on the Volga. It is one of the main written sources for the peoples of the 10th-century steppe.</p></div>
